+### #529 — riscv64 smp_multicpu counter gap: RFENCE success uncounted (FIXED, gates green)
+
+- **Learned:** (1) The "IPI never lands" title was wrong — GDB batch (silent printf+continue breakpoints) showed exactly one SSWI (bring-up loopback) and zero APPLY/send-failure hits across both failing tests: RFENCE succeeds synchronously in firmware so no IPI is ever needed, and the counter only advanced in `apply_shootdowns`. Observability gap, not delivery failure. (2) Before theorizing about trap enablement/masks/mailbox visibility (H2-H6), instrument the three dispatch outcomes (RFENCE-ok / RFENCE-fail / mailbox-apply) — one GDB run with three silent breakpoints decided it in 30s, ruling out five hypotheses at once. (3) Weak-import test hooks declared-but-never-called (`set_sbi_rfence_for_test`) are a premise smell: the test intends to exercise a path it never forces — flag it in the issue for the testbed lane instead of assuming the kernel path is broken. (4) riscv64 `smp` class has zero registered tests (policy arch_scope=x86_64) — the harness idles into a watchdog TIMEOUT; verify policy before chasing a "hang" in an empty class (baseline prints the same "No tests registered").
+- **Adapted:** `note_shootdown_applied()` in smp.cpp/hpp (mirrors `note_shootdown_send_failure` + aarch64's defined-but-uncalled precedent), +1 on RFENCE `error==0` with the synchronicity rationale in the comment; counter stays observability-only. Testbed handoff: force the mailbox fallback in the rv64 legs to lock the S-soft path permanently.
+- **Style re-face:** when a counter assert fails but all functional asserts pass, suspect the counter's coverage before the mechanism — enumerate every increment site vs every completion path; a fast path that bypasses the instrumented slow path is an accounting bug until proven otherwise.
+
 ### #526 — riscv64 split-L2 teardown leak: get_table split ignored user_alloc (FIXED user-half, gates green)
 
 - **Learned:** `get_table()`'s split branch hardcoded `alloc_page_table()` (KERNEL-owned) while its create branch honored `user_alloc` — and x86's inline split already used `alloc_user_page()`. So riscv64 user-map splits (no inline split in its `map_page_in_pml4_try`; relies on `get_table`) produced KERNEL-owned L2s that `free_user_pages` skips by owner-bit (`vmm_teardown.cpp:107`) → PMM+1 per split. The kernel-half +2 is a different animal: `free_user_pages` never walks `L0[256+]`, so no ownership fixes it — that half is test-side manual restore (mirroring the x86 branch already in the test) or a gated unmap-reclaim, both tracked for testbed. `PMM::free_page` idempotency + x86's `alloc_user_page` split precedent made the one-line ownership parity provably safe (no pool-overlay interaction: user splits live outside the table-pool machinery on all arches).
